1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,980
 Hello. In this tutorial I'm gonna show you my thought process when I animate my camera. I'm gonna use one of my earlier tutorials as an example. So here is the project for a tutorial I made last fall in 2025. It's called Squelch Snare and in this tutorial I show you how to create a squelch type sounding snare. But anyway, if I select the camera here, you can see all the keyframes for the camera. So here I have the dope sheet and the camera selected and if you want to go to the dope sheet,

2
00:00:29,980 --> 00:00:59,960
 you can just select this and choose dope sheet. So here is essentially all the keyframes for the camera. So as you can see I have plenty of keyframes here. We can just scroll through the whole scene here. So it ends at 2700 frames and begins at 500 frames. So we can just check out the first keyframe here. Just to know here if I select to the left here, object transforms, you can see the actual transforming keyframes for the camera movements. So here is the X location.

3
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:17,120
 the Y location and the Z location. And down here if I click on this drop down, you can see the focal length and focus distance and f-stop. Which is if I go to the camera here to the right and go down to depth of field are these ones.

4
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:32,460
 And these are like just the keyframe, the focus, distance and the f-stop. So right now we're going to focus on object transforms only. So I'm going to close this drop down and you can just ignore the keyframes on the very bottom here and just focus on these three.

5
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:48,460
 When I begin my tutorials, I like to begin at the first frame with the keyframe. And just one note, is that I animate everything in 60fps, so depending on what frame range you're using, for example, if you're animating in 30fps, you would need to recalculate all the frames.

6
00:01:48,460 --> 00:02:00,460
 So for example, 9 frames in 60fps would equal around 4 or 5 frames in 30fps, since 30fps is half of 60. So I will show everything in 60fps today.

7
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:17,460
 Anyway, on the first frame, we have the camera here. And if we scroll forward, you can see that I just keyframe it a bit back. So if we go from the very side here, and I move forward, you can see that the camera actually begins by moving backwards.

8
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:26,460
 And that's because I want to create an illusion that the camera gets momentum for them to go forward, as you can see.

9
00:02:26,460 --> 00:02:34,460
 So the camera begins by moving backwards and then it shoots forward. Again.

10
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:42,460
 But what happens when it has come forward? It then moves a bit backwards. And doing this, I want to capture like a realistic feel to the camera.

11
00:02:42,460 --> 00:02:50,460
 Because as if you were throwing a ball, for example, you would first move your hand backwards, and then move it forward, and then you would pick your hand up.

12
00:02:50,460 --> 00:03:02,460
 So I want to reflect the momentum to make the camera movement feel realistic. Because for example, if the camera just stopped there, and never grabbed momentum back, it would feel more static and not as bouncy.

13
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:09,460
 So if I scroll through the whole motion here, you can see, first goes back, then forward, and then it falls back a bit.

14
00:03:11,460 --> 00:03:18,460
 So that's essentially the whole philosophy behind my camera animations, and it's super standard in any type of animation.

15
00:03:18,460 --> 00:03:20,460
 So we can see what happens next.

16
00:03:20,460 --> 00:03:22,460
 So again, I scroll forward.

17
00:03:22,460 --> 00:03:28,460
 And, as you can see, now the camera is almost like floating.

18
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:34,460
 The keyframe that makes it floating is actually this keyframe in the middle between those two.

19
00:03:34,460 --> 00:03:37,460
 So we can try to delete this keyframe.

20
00:03:37,460 --> 00:03:45,460
 If I select the Orbit Transform, and I select this keyframe, it will actually select all the axes down here.

21
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:52,460
 So if I select on the first row here, it will automatically select Z, Y, and X axes.

22
00:03:52,460 --> 00:03:58,460
 So I select this, and press X to delete it, and now you can see that the camera actually stays in place.

23
00:03:58,460 --> 00:04:02,460
 So we can watch this in real time, if you watch it on the left here.

24
00:04:05,460 --> 00:04:08,460
 You can see that the camera stays completely still here.

25
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:17,460
 I didn't even want that, and that's why I added the keyframes here that we just deleted.

26
00:04:17,460 --> 00:04:20,460
 So I'm going to undo it and get them back.

27
00:04:20,460 --> 00:04:22,460
 Now we can watch it.

28
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:28,460
 So you can see, we just have some floating motion here.

29
00:04:28,460 --> 00:04:36,460
 And if I delete it again, you can see it's very static.

30
00:04:36,460 --> 00:04:41,460
 So what I'm essentially doing is that if I have a piece of my animation where the camera needs to be still,

31
00:04:41,460 --> 00:04:43,460
 I will just create this type of floating motion.

32
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:46,460
 So what I do is that I go to the middle of the keyframes.

33
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:50,460
 So here's the first keyframe for the camera, and here's the second.

34
00:04:50,460 --> 00:04:55,460
 You can just ignore this as I said, because it's for the f-stop and the focus animation.

35
00:04:55,460 --> 00:05:01,460
 So this again, first keyframe and second keyframe.

36
00:05:01,460 --> 00:05:05,460
 I put my cursor around the middle, and I select the camera.

37
00:05:05,460 --> 00:05:10,460
 And then I just press G and Z, and move it down just a little bit.

38
00:05:11,460 --> 00:05:16,460
 And then I press enter, and then I press K, and select location keyframe.

39
00:05:16,460 --> 00:05:20,460
 And now I've created this almost floating type animation.

40
00:05:20,460 --> 00:05:26,460
 So it goes down, and then up again to the original position.

41
00:05:26,460 --> 00:05:30,460
 So we can see the full animation again.

42
00:05:33,460 --> 00:05:34,460
 Very nice.

43
00:05:34,460 --> 00:05:35,460
 But what happens next?

44
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:41,460
 Because now the camera is going to zoom in to the squelchner here.

45
00:05:41,460 --> 00:05:46,460
 So again, we are having the same philosophy in mind, where the camera is going back a bit.

46
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:48,460
 And then it shoots forward.

47
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:51,460
 And then it bounces back.

48
00:05:51,460 --> 00:05:54,460
 And then actually a bit forward again.

49
00:05:54,460 --> 00:05:58,460
 So watch the camera closely as I scroll through these keyframes here.

50
00:05:58,460 --> 00:06:02,460
 First, it's on its original position.

51
00:06:02,460 --> 00:06:07,460
 Then it gathers momentum by going back.

52
00:06:07,460 --> 00:06:10,460
 Then it shoots very fast forward.

53
00:06:10,460 --> 00:06:12,460
 And then it bounces back.

54
00:06:12,460 --> 00:06:15,460
 And then actually it bounces forward a bit again.

55
00:06:15,460 --> 00:06:21,460
 So that's essentially the exact philosophy as I had here at the beginning.

56
00:06:22,460 --> 00:06:23,460
 So watch it again.

57
00:06:23,460 --> 00:06:31,460
 So we can watch the full sequence here.

58
00:06:31,460 --> 00:06:37,460
 That's essentially me repeating this thing, but here.

59
00:06:37,460 --> 00:06:40,460
 And you might recognize this here.

60
00:06:40,460 --> 00:06:44,460
 Because in the middle here, we have the exact same thing as we have here.

61
00:06:45,460 --> 00:06:57,460
 If I delete this, you can see that the camera, if I scroll here from 699 up to this keyframe.

62
00:06:57,460 --> 00:07:02,460
 And if you watch the camera, you can see that the camera stands completely still.

63
00:07:02,460 --> 00:07:06,460
 And I again wanted the floating motion that I had before here.

64
00:07:06,460 --> 00:07:10,460
 So that's why I have had this keyframe in the middle here.

65
00:07:11,460 --> 00:07:14,460
 So you can see, the camera move goes a bit down.

66
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:18,460
 Then it goes up.

67
00:07:18,460 --> 00:07:21,460
 So if I delete it, I can show you how I did it.

68
00:07:21,460 --> 00:07:23,460
 It's the exact same thing as I've shown you before.

69
00:07:23,460 --> 00:07:26,460
 So I go to the middle of these two keyframes.

70
00:07:26,460 --> 00:07:29,460
 The first one and the second one here.

71
00:07:30,460 --> 00:07:32,460
 I select and put the cursor around the middle.

72
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:34,460
 Then I make sure the camera is selected.

73
00:07:34,460 --> 00:07:35,460
 I press G and Z.

74
00:07:35,460 --> 00:07:39,460
 And just move it down a little bit to create the floating motion.

75
00:07:39,460 --> 00:07:41,460
 And then I have pressed enter.

76
00:07:41,460 --> 00:07:45,460
 And press K and location to insert keyframe.

77
00:07:45,460 --> 00:07:49,460
 So now you can see that the camera is going down and just up a little bit.

78
00:07:49,460 --> 00:07:51,460
 Just to make the camera movement come alive.

79
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:53,460
 And what happens here.

80
00:07:53,460 --> 00:07:56,460
 We can see the camera is actually moving forward a bit.

81
00:07:56,460 --> 00:07:57,460
 So you can see.

82
00:07:57,460 --> 00:07:58,460
 We see it again.

83
00:07:58,460 --> 00:08:00,460
 Scrolling forward.

84
00:08:00,460 --> 00:08:03,460
 Of course it goes toward the object.

85
00:08:03,460 --> 00:08:06,460
 Before it shoots back.

86
00:08:06,460 --> 00:08:11,460
 And then it goes towards the object a little bit again.

87
00:08:12,460 --> 00:08:17,460
 And you might recognize that this is the exact same philosophy as I had here.

88
00:08:17,460 --> 00:08:18,460
 And at the beginning.

89
00:08:18,460 --> 00:08:22,460
 So again the camera is going towards the object to gather momentum.

90
00:08:22,460 --> 00:08:24,460
 Then it shoots back.

91
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:27,460
 Then it catches its momentum by going forward a bit.

92
00:08:27,460 --> 00:08:30,460
 Just like when you throw a ball.

93
00:08:30,460 --> 00:08:32,460
 It's the exact same type of thinking.

94
00:08:32,460 --> 00:08:35,460
 And again the camera is just floating here.

95
00:08:35,460 --> 00:08:37,460
 As we did here.

96
00:08:37,460 --> 00:08:39,460
 This keyframe makes the camera go up.

97
00:08:39,460 --> 00:08:40,460
 And then down again.

98
00:08:41,460 --> 00:08:42,460
 So yeah.

99
00:08:42,460 --> 00:08:45,460
 That's essentially everything I make for my camera animations.

100
00:08:45,460 --> 00:08:47,460
 Because as you can see.

101
00:08:47,460 --> 00:08:48,460
 Here again.

102
00:08:48,460 --> 00:08:50,460
 It goes forward.

103
00:08:50,460 --> 00:08:52,460
 And then it shoots backwards.

104
00:08:52,460 --> 00:08:56,460
 Goes forward.

105
00:08:56,460 --> 00:08:57,460
 Shoots backwards.

106
00:08:57,460 --> 00:08:59,460
 And then forward just a little bit.

107
00:08:59,460 --> 00:09:03,460
 So you can think of it as the four keyframe rule.

108
00:09:03,460 --> 00:09:08,460
 Where the actual important movement happens between second and third keyframe.

109
00:09:10,460 --> 00:09:15,460
 But the movement and the momentum is gathered between the first and second keyframe.

110
00:09:15,460 --> 00:09:20,460
 And the movement is ending between the third and fourth keyframe.

111
00:09:20,460 --> 00:09:25,460
 So you can see the actual movement is happening between the second and the third.

112
00:09:26,460 --> 00:09:27,460
 But the momentum is gathered.

113
00:09:27,460 --> 00:09:31,460
 But the momentum is gathered between the first and the second one.

114
00:09:31,460 --> 00:09:35,460
 And it's ending between the third and the fourth one.

115
00:09:35,460 --> 00:09:41,460
 But what you would need to do when you change the location of the camera.

116
00:09:41,460 --> 00:09:45,460
 And if you have depth of field selected as I have here to the right.

117
00:09:46,460 --> 00:09:50,460
 What is important is the keyframe the focus distance and the f-stop together with the camera movements.

118
00:09:50,460 --> 00:09:52,460
 So as I said before.

119
00:09:52,460 --> 00:09:54,460
 Down here.

120
00:09:54,460 --> 00:09:56,460
 We have the focus distance animation.

121
00:09:56,460 --> 00:10:00,460
 And I have actually synced this up perfectly with the camera movement.

122
00:10:00,460 --> 00:10:05,460
 So almost on every camera keyframe I have a keyframe for the focus distance.

123
00:10:06,460 --> 00:10:10,460
 For example we can try to delete all the focus keyframe distances here.

124
00:10:10,460 --> 00:10:15,460
 You will see that everything becomes blurry.

125
00:10:15,460 --> 00:10:19,460
 What's happening is that the focus distance is not animated together with the focal length.

126
00:10:20,460 --> 00:10:23,460
 So to fix that you would need to go to every keyframe here.

127
00:10:23,460 --> 00:10:26,460
 So if I go to this keyframe, the second keyframe of the animation.

128
00:10:26,460 --> 00:10:28,460
 I would then zoom in on the object.

129
00:10:28,460 --> 00:10:30,460
 Make sure the camera was selected.

130
00:10:30,460 --> 00:10:33,460
 And then go to the right here.

131
00:10:33,460 --> 00:10:38,460
 And just scroll on the focus distance until I see that the object is in focus.

132
00:10:38,460 --> 00:10:42,460
 So for example here I see that the object is in focus.

133
00:10:42,460 --> 00:10:45,460
 When I'm satisfied with the focus, right there.

134
00:10:46,460 --> 00:10:51,460
 I will then press hover over the focus distance and press I to insert the keyframe.

135
00:10:51,460 --> 00:10:54,460
 And then I will go to the next keyframe of the camera.

136
00:10:54,460 --> 00:10:57,460
 You can see that it's a bit blurry here.

137
00:10:57,460 --> 00:10:59,460
 I want some more focus here.

138
00:10:59,460 --> 00:11:02,460
 So then I would need to make sure the camera is selected.

139
00:11:02,460 --> 00:11:04,460
 Go to the focus distance.

140
00:11:04,460 --> 00:11:07,460
 Hold shift while scrolling to make fine adjustments.

141
00:11:07,460 --> 00:11:11,460
 And now you can see that we are on focus here.

142
00:11:11,460 --> 00:11:14,460
 You see, it's almost in focus.

143
00:11:15,460 --> 00:11:16,460
 I think that would be enough.

144
00:11:16,460 --> 00:11:21,460
 So then again I will hover over this and press I to insert the keyframe down here.

145
00:11:21,460 --> 00:11:25,460
 And then I will just go to the next keyframe.

146
00:11:25,460 --> 00:11:27,460
 Zoom in.

147
00:11:27,460 --> 00:11:30,460
 And now you see that we are a bit out of focus here.

148
00:11:30,460 --> 00:11:33,460
 You can see it on the pixel here.

149
00:11:33,460 --> 00:11:35,460
 So I will go to the focus distance.

150
00:11:35,460 --> 00:11:38,460
 And just scroll it.

151
00:11:38,460 --> 00:11:40,460
 Make sure we are on focus.

152
00:11:40,460 --> 00:11:42,460
 And now we have some sharp pixels here.

153
00:11:42,460 --> 00:11:45,460
 So again hover over it and press I.

154
00:11:45,460 --> 00:11:47,460
 And then go to the next keyframe.

155
00:11:47,460 --> 00:11:49,460
 Make sure we are on focus.

156
00:11:49,460 --> 00:11:51,460
 And I think we are fairly in focus here.

157
00:11:51,460 --> 00:11:55,460
 So I will just hover over this and press I.

158
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:58,460
 Go to the next keyframe.

159
00:11:58,460 --> 00:12:00,460
 Do the same thing.

160
00:12:01,460 --> 00:12:05,460
 So that's essentially how you link up your focus distance with your focal length.

161
00:12:05,460 --> 00:12:07,460
 And it's a tedious process.

162
00:12:07,460 --> 00:12:09,460
 But I think it's worth it.

163
00:12:09,460 --> 00:12:11,460
 And you are going to get faster on it when you get the hang of it.

164
00:12:11,460 --> 00:12:15,460
 And the next thing I want to show you is how I get the camera to be synced with the objects on screen.

165
00:12:16,460 --> 00:12:17,460
 So here you have the camera.

166
00:12:17,460 --> 00:12:20,460
 But it's not actually looking towards the object here.

167
00:12:20,460 --> 00:12:23,460
 Rather it's looking towards this empty object.

168
00:12:23,460 --> 00:12:25,460
 As you can see here.

169
00:12:25,460 --> 00:12:28,460
 So if I grab this empty object and move it around.

170
00:12:28,460 --> 00:12:34,460
 You can see to the left here that the camera is changing where it looks depending on where I put this.

171
00:12:35,460 --> 00:12:37,460
 What I have is that I have on the camera.

172
00:12:37,460 --> 00:12:39,460
 And go to the constraints.

173
00:12:39,460 --> 00:12:40,460
 I have a track to constraints.

174
00:12:40,460 --> 00:12:42,460
 Where the empty is selected.

175
00:12:42,460 --> 00:12:46,460
 So I can just close this down and do it again.

176
00:12:46,460 --> 00:12:48,460
 So I go to add object constraints.

177
00:12:48,460 --> 00:12:51,460
 And then I select track to.

178
00:12:51,460 --> 00:12:54,460
 And then while the camera is selected.

179
00:12:54,460 --> 00:12:56,460
 I go to the object.

180
00:12:56,460 --> 00:12:57,460
 I want to track the camera to.

181
00:12:57,460 --> 00:12:59,460
 So here I see the object.

182
00:12:59,460 --> 00:13:00,460
 I want to track the camera to.

183
00:13:00,460 --> 00:13:02,460
 It's an empty object.

184
00:13:03,460 --> 00:13:04,460
 Just get an empty object by.

185
00:13:04,460 --> 00:13:07,460
 Pressing shift A and search for an empty sphere.

186
00:13:07,460 --> 00:13:09,460
 So select the camera.

187
00:13:09,460 --> 00:13:11,460
 And then choose the drop down.

188
00:13:11,460 --> 00:13:13,460
 And select the empty object.

189
00:13:13,460 --> 00:13:17,460
 And what's good when you track the camera to empty objects.

190
00:13:17,460 --> 00:13:20,460
 Is that the empty objects won't show up in the final render.

191
00:13:20,460 --> 00:13:22,460
 So you can have this anywhere on the screen.

192
00:13:22,460 --> 00:13:24,460
 And it won't show up in the render.

193
00:13:24,460 --> 00:13:25,460
 It's invisible.

194
00:13:25,460 --> 00:13:28,460
 Which makes it very good to utilize for your animations.

195
00:13:28,460 --> 00:13:31,460
 And now I have the empty object selected here.

196
00:13:31,460 --> 00:13:32,460
 You can barely see it.

197
00:13:32,460 --> 00:13:34,460
 Because it's almost in the object here.

198
00:13:34,460 --> 00:13:35,460
 As you can see.

199
00:13:35,460 --> 00:13:38,460
 I have all these keyframes for the actual empty object.

200
00:13:38,460 --> 00:13:40,460
 If we go to the first frame of the animation.

201
00:13:40,460 --> 00:13:42,460
 Which is frame 500.

202
00:13:42,460 --> 00:13:45,460
 You can see that the empty object is almost in the middle of the scene.

203
00:13:45,460 --> 00:13:46,460
 And then we go forward a bit.

204
00:13:46,460 --> 00:13:49,460
 We can see that it stays the same.

205
00:13:49,460 --> 00:13:51,460
 Because when you have these lines going.

206
00:13:51,460 --> 00:13:53,460
 Between keyframes.

207
00:13:53,460 --> 00:13:55,460
 It means that the keyframes are identical.

208
00:13:55,460 --> 00:13:57,460
 So these keyframes are identical.

209
00:13:57,460 --> 00:13:59,460
 But here.

210
00:13:59,460 --> 00:14:01,460
 We can see that.

211
00:14:01,460 --> 00:14:03,460
 It's no lines between the keyframes.

212
00:14:03,460 --> 00:14:07,460
 Which means that the movement of the sphere is changing.

213
00:14:07,460 --> 00:14:08,460
 If you just zoom in here.

214
00:14:08,460 --> 00:14:10,460
 You can see the empty object.

215
00:14:10,460 --> 00:14:13,460
 And I'm going to scroll towards the next keyframe.

216
00:14:13,460 --> 00:14:15,460
 And we can see how it changes its position.

217
00:14:16,460 --> 00:14:22,460
 You can see that it goes a bit left.

218
00:14:22,460 --> 00:14:27,460
 I can scale it up so you can see it better.

219
00:14:27,460 --> 00:14:29,460
 This moves a bit left.

220
00:14:29,460 --> 00:14:32,460
 And that's because at the beginning of the scene.

221
00:14:32,460 --> 00:14:36,460
 I want the viewer to focus on the washing machine here.

222
00:14:36,460 --> 00:14:39,460
 So you can see that it's almost in the center of the washing machine.

223
00:14:39,460 --> 00:14:40,460
 But when I come up here.

224
00:14:40,460 --> 00:14:43,460
 I want the viewer to focus in.

225
00:14:43,460 --> 00:14:44,460
 On this audio file here.

226
00:14:44,460 --> 00:14:47,460
 So that's why I need to change the position of the sphere.

227
00:14:47,460 --> 00:14:48,460
 Because for example.

228
00:14:48,460 --> 00:14:51,460
 If I didn't do the little tilt to the left here.

229
00:14:51,460 --> 00:14:53,460
 It would just look like this.

230
00:14:53,460 --> 00:14:57,460
 You see that it would look a bit off here to the left.

231
00:14:57,460 --> 00:14:59,460
 But when I had this little change here.

232
00:14:59,460 --> 00:15:00,460
 With the keyframes.

233
00:15:00,460 --> 00:15:03,460
 You can see that the center of attention is the audio file here.

234
00:15:03,460 --> 00:15:05,460
